id: magma-environment
name: Magma Environment
kind: battlefield
environment: true
unbreathable: true
hardnessMin: 4
hardnessMax: 4
qualities: aflame
source: Core Rule
description: >
  Deep molten rock. Unbreathable and Aflame, and ending your turn in it is two stacks of
  Broken until your next turn begins. Hardness Rank 4.
text: >
  Magma Environment: A harsh environment with deep pools of underground molten rock,
  where the amount is enough to submerge even a giant.

  Hardness Rank: 4

  Effects: This Battle Environment has multiple effects:

  * This is an Unbreathable Environment.

  * This Environment possesses the Aflame Environmental Quality.

  * If you end your Turn in this Battle Environment, gain 2 stacks of the Broken Combat
  Condition until the start of your next turn.

---

# The Lava Environment with the water's problem on top: you cannot breathe it, and it burns
# twice as fast. Hardness Rank 4 rather than 3, which is the difference between standing on
# molten rock and being in it.
#
# --- What needed nothing built ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# "This is an Unbreathable Environment" is `unbreathable: true`, and every piece already
# reads it there - the hook that watches entering and leaving, the Survival Check on the
# tab, the Combat Round and the Health Threshold that each take a stack of Held Breath.
#
# "This Environment possesses the Aflame Environmental Quality" is `qualities: aflame`,
# carried and read by nobody, the same as the Lava Environment's. Environmental Qualities
# are their own list and it has not arrived.
#
# --- Two stacks, on two clocks -----------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# "Gain 2 stacks of the Broken Combat Condition until the start of your next turn."
#
# Two, which found the mirror of a bug the Lava Environment found. `gain` set a Condition
# rather than adding to it, and a clock on a Condition cleared it rather than taking a stack
# off - so two stacks gained here would have come off together, and taken a third with them
# if anything else had put one on.
#
# Both are one stack at a time now, which is the rule a Resource has always followed. The
# number after the duration is how many clocks to set: two stacks gained together need two,
# or the second stays on for ever.
#
# At Broken's cap of three, two a turn means a character who ends two turns in the magma is
# at three and stays there while they are in it - the third clock running out as the fourth
# and fifth are set. Which is the rule working, not the cap papering over it.

[automatic/end-of-turn]
gain(broken, 2);
expires(broken, start-of-next-turn, 2);
